## What?
PR updates block type processing helper function and sets the default - `apiVersion: 1`.

## Why?
* Ensures `apiVersion` is always defined during block registration.
* Matches the defaults provided by the server. Sources: [one](https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/d6b7ae50e532749c7edbe26a6a9ccef94a98ff59/src/wp-includes/class-wp-block-type.php#L20-L26) and [two](https://github.com/WordPress/wordpress-develop/blob/d6b7ae50e532749c7edbe26a6a9ccef94a98ff59/src/wp-includes/rest-api/endpoints/class-wp-rest-block-types-controller.php#L484-L490).

## Testing Instructions
1. Open a post or page.
2. Do a client-side block registration via the `registerBlockType` function.
3. Confirm that the registered block has the default `apiVersion` set - `wp.blocks.getBlockType( 'test/block-api-version' );`.

### Example Block

```js
const { createElement: el } = wp.element;
const { registerBlockType } = wp.blocks;
const { useBlockProps } = wp.blockEditor;

registerBlockType( 'test/block-api-version', {
	apiVersion: 3,
	title: 'Test Block: apiVersion',
	icon: 'list-view',
	category: 'text',

	edit: function Edit() {
		const blockProps = useBlockProps();

		return el(
			'div',
			{ ...blockProps },
			'hello'
		);
	},
} );
```

### Testing Instructions for Keyboard
Same.

## Problem

The `SVGVEleProps` interface was missing the `key` property, causing TypeScript compilation errors when the `SvgEle` component was used in React map operations.

**Error details:**
```
src/svg/svgChart.tsx:212:41 - error TS2322: Type '{ key: string; node: SVGVNode; }' is not assignable to type 'SVGVEleProps'.
  Property 'key' does not exist on type 'SVGVEleProps'.

212       {children?.map((child) => <SvgEle key={child.key} node={child} />)}
                                            ~~~
```

## Solution

Added the optional `key?: string` property to the `SVGVEleProps` interface to align with React's requirement for `key` props in mapped components.

**Before:**
```typescript
interface SVGVEleProps {
  node: SVGVNode;
  touchStart?: any;
  touchMove?: any;
  touchEnd?: any;
}
```

**After:**
```typescript
interface SVGVEleProps {
  node: SVGVNode;
  key?: string;
  touchStart?: any;
  touchMove?: any;
  touchEnd?: any;
}
```

## Testing

- ✅ TypeScript compilation now passes without errors (`yarn typescript`)
- ✅ Build process completes successfully (`yarn prepare`)
- ✅ No functional changes to component behavior
- ✅ Maintains backwards compatibility

This is a minimal fix that resolves the type definition mismatch while preserving all existing functionality.

